        "installation_method": "python -m pip install -r requirements.txt",
        "intended_users": [
          "administrator",
          "API consumers",
          "web users"
        ],
        "original_user_request": "PROJECT: BEAUTY LIB\n\nCreate a production-oriented cross-platform mobile application for beauty salons called:\n\nBeauty Lip\n\nThe product should provide a booking experience similar in concept to modern doctor/salon appointment applications:\n\n- discover salons and beauty professionals\n- browse services\n- see real availability\n- book an appointment\n- reschedule or cancel\n- receive reminders\n- manage appointments\n\nDo not copy the branding, copyrighted interface, text, assets, source code, or exact visual identity of any existing application.\n\nCreate an original Beauty Lip product with its own architecture, UI design, branding, and user experience.\n\nThe initial target platforms are:\n\n- Android\n- iOS\n\nThe architecture must also include:\n\n- backend API\n- persistent database\n- salon/business management interface\n- administrator functionality\n\nThe first version must be a functional MVP, not a static design prototype.\n\n---\n\n# 1. PRODUCT PURPOSE\n\nBeauty Lip is a mobile marketplace and appointment-management application for:\n\n- beauty salons\n- hair salons\n- nail studios\n- brow and lash studios\n- cosmetology studios\n- makeup artists\n- massage and wellness studios\n- independent beauty professionals\n\nCustomers should be able to find a salon or specialist and book an available appointment without calling.\n\nSalons should be able to manage:\n\n- branches\n- specialists\n- services\n- prices\n- schedules\n- bookings\n- customers\n- notifications\n\n---\n\n# 2. USER ROLES\n\nImplement these roles:\n\n## Customer\n\nA customer can:\n\n- register and log in\n- browse salons\n- search and filter salons\n- view specialists\n- view services and prices\n- see available appointment times\n- book an appointment\n- reschedule an appointment\n- cancel an appointment\n- see upcoming and previous appointments\n- save favorite salons and specialists\n- leave a rating and review after a completed appointment\n- manage profile and notification settings\n\n## Beauty Professional\n\nA professional can:\n\n- view assigned appointments\n- view daily and weekly schedule\n- view customer and service details\n- mark appointment status\n- configure personal working hours if permitted\n- mark breaks and unavailable periods\n- view basic performance statistics\n\n## Salon Manager\n\nA salon manager can:\n\n- create and edit salon profile\n- manage salon branches\n- manage specialists\n- create and edit services\n- set prices and service duration\n- configure working hours\n- manage bookings\n- block unavailable time\n- view customers\n- view business statistics\n- manage salon photos\n- manage notification settings\n\n## Platform Administrator\n\nAn administrator can:\n\n- view and manage all salons\n- approve, suspend, or reject salons\n- manage user accounts\n- moderate reviews\n- manage service categories\n- view system statistics\n- inspect audit logs\n- manage reported content\n\nUse role-based access control.\n\nDo not allow customers to access salon-management or administrator functionality.\n\n---\n\n# 3. CUSTOMER MOBILE APPLICATION\n\nCreate these main mobile screens:\n\n## Splash Screen\n\nShow:\n\n- Beauty Lip logo\n- short loading state\n- authentication/session restoration\n\n## Onboarding\n\nThree concise slides:\n\n1. Find trusted salons and professionals.\n2. Book available appointments instantly.\n3. Manage all beauty appointments in one place.\n\nInclude:\n\n- Skip\n- Next\n- Get Started\n\nShow onboarding only for first launch unless reset.\n\n## Authentication\n\nSupport:\n\n- email and password registration\n- email and password login\n- password reset\n- logout\n- session persistence\n\nPrepare architecture for future:\n\n- Google login\n- Apple login\n- phone-number login\n\nDo not require social authentication in the first MVP.\n\n## Home\n\nShow:\n\n- current city or location\n- search field\n- service categories\n- recommended salons\n- nearby salons\n- popular specialists\n- upcoming appointment summary\n- promotions section if available\n\nDo not fill production views with fake hardcoded data.\n\nUse backend data and show proper empty/loading/error states.\n\n## Search\n\nAllow searching by:\n\n- salon name\n- specialist name\n- service name\n- category\n- city or district\n\nFilters:\n\n- distance\n- rating\n- price range\n- available today\n- available this week\n- service category\n- salon or independent professional\n- gender preference where legally and product-wise appropriate\n\nSorting:\n\n- recommended\n- nearest\n- highest rated\n- lowest price\n- earliest available\n\n## Map\n\nProvide map-ready architecture for salons and professionals.\n\nFirst implementation may use:\n\n- OpenStreetMap-compatible mapping\n- another properly configured map provider\n\nDo not hardcode a paid map provider without configuration.\n\nShow:\n\n- salon markers\n- current map area\n- selected salon preview\n- list/map switch\n\nIf map API credentials are unavailable:\n\n- keep map integration optional\n- show a clear configuration requirement\n- do not block the rest of the application\n\n## Salon Profile\n\nDisplay:\n\n- salon name\n- logo and cover image\n- gallery\n- address\n- map location\n- phone\n- opening hours\n- rating\n- review count\n- description\n- facilities and amenities\n- specialists\n- service categories\n- service list\n- prices\n- duration\n- earliest available time\n- favorite action\n- booking action\n\n## Specialist Profile\n\nDisplay:\n\n- photo\n- name\n- title/specialization\n- experience\n- biography\n- salon\n- services\n- prices\n- rating\n- portfolio\n- available time slots\n- booking action\n\n## Service Selection\n\nThe customer chooses:\n\n1. salon or specialist\n2. service category\n3. service\n4. optional specialist\n5. date\n6. available time\n7. booking confirmation\n\nShow clearly:\n\n- price\n- duration\n- address\n- cancellation policy\n- specialist\n- selected date and time\n\n## Appointment Booking\n\nBooking must be transactional.\n\nPrevent:\n\n- double booking\n- booking unavailable slots\n- overlapping appointments for one specialist\n- booking outside working hours\n- booking during breaks or blocked time\n\nAfter confirmation:\n\n- create appointment\n- reserve the time slot\n- display booking confirmation\n- add the appointment to customer history\n- notify the salon side\n\n## My Appointments\n\nSections:\n\n- Upcoming\n- Completed\n- Cancelled\n\nEach appointment displays:\n\n- salon\n- specialist\n- service\n- date\n- time\n- duration\n- price\n- status\n- address\n\nAvailable actions according to state:\n\n- open details\n- reschedule\n- cancel\n- contact salon\n- add to calendar\n- leave review after completion\n\n## Appointment Details\n\nShow:\n\n- booking ID\n- service\n- salon\n- specialist\n- date/time\n- duration\n- price\n- status history\n- cancellation policy\n- salon address\n- contact details\n\n## Favorites\n\nAllow saving:\n\n- salons\n- specialists\n\nFavorites must persist in the database.\n\n## Reviews\n\nAfter a completed appointment, allow:\n\n- rating from 1 to 5\n- optional text review\n\nPrevent:\n\n- review before completion\n- multiple active reviews for the same appointment\n- review of another customer’s appointment\n\nSalon managers must not be able to edit customer reviews.\n\nAdministrators may moderate reviews.\n\n## Customer Profile\n\nInclude:\n\n- name\n- email\n- phone\n- profile image\n- preferred city\n- language\n- notification preferences\n- privacy settings\n- password change\n- account deletion request\n\n---\n\n# 4. SALON MANAGEMENT APPLICATION\n\nThe salon-management experience may initially be implemented as:\n\n- responsive web application\n- tablet-friendly web dashboard\n- dedicated manager section\n\nPrepare the architecture for a future separate manager mobile application.\n\nRequired pages:\n\n## Manager Dashboard\n\nShow:\n\n- today’s appointments\n- appointments awaiting confirmation\n- completed appointments\n- cancellations\n- today’s expected revenue\n- upcoming schedule\n- available time gaps\n\n## Calendar\n\nViews:\n\n- day\n- week\n- specialist\n\nSupport:\n\n- appointment creation\n- appointment update\n- appointment cancellation\n- drag/drop only if implemented reliably\n- blocked times\n- breaks\n- specialist filters\n\n## Appointments\n\nAllow:\n\n- view\n- create\n- edit\n- confirm\n- cancel\n- mark no-show\n- mark completed\n- add internal notes\n\nAppointment statuses:\n\n- pending\n- confirmed\n- in_progress\n- completed\n- cancelled_by_customer\n- cancelled_by_salon\n- no_show\n\nStatus transitions must be validated.\n\n## Specialists\n\nSalon managers can:\n\n- add specialist\n- edit specialist\n- deactivate specialist\n- upload profile image\n- assign services\n- set working schedule\n- configure breaks\n- configure days off\n\nDo not permanently delete specialists with booking history.\n\nUse soft deletion/deactivation.\n\n## Services\n\nFields:\n\n- service name\n- category\n- description\n- duration in minutes\n- base price\n- optional price range\n- active status\n- specialists who provide it\n- preparation or aftercare instructions\n\n## Customers\n\nDisplay:\n\n- customer name\n- contact information\n- appointment count\n- last appointment\n- notes\n- no-show count\n\nProtect personal information according to role permissions.\n\n## Salon Profile\n\nFields:\n\n- name\n- description\n- address\n- coordinates\n- contact details\n- working hours\n- logo\n- gallery\n- amenities\n- cancellation policy\n- booking lead time\n- minimum cancellation notice\n\n## Analytics\n\nMVP statistics:\n\n- appointments by day/week/month\n- completed appointments\n- cancellations\n- no-shows\n- revenue estimate\n- most popular services\n- busiest specialists\n- returning customers\n\nDo not present estimated values as settled financial records.\n\n---\n\n# 5. BOOKING AND AVAILABILITY ENGINE\n\nThis is a critical part of the application.\n\nAvailability must be calculated using:\n\n- salon opening hours\n- specialist working hours\n- specialist breaks\n- specialist days off\n- existing appointments\n- service duration\n- optional buffer before appointment\n- optional buffer after appointment\n- booking lead time\n- maximum future booking window\n\nExample:\n\nSpecialist works:\n\n09:00–18:00\n\nBreak:\n\n13:00–14:00\n\nSelected service duration:\n\n60 minutes\n\nExisting appointment:\n\n10:00–11:00\n\nAvailable times must not overlap the appointment or break.\n\nUse server-side validation when creating or rescheduling a booking.\n\nClient-side availability display is not sufficient.\n\nPrevent race conditions using:\n\n- database transaction\n- locking strategy\n- unique/exclusion constraints where supported\n- final availability check immediately before booking commit\n\n---\n\n# 6. NOTIFICATIONS\n\nImplement a notification abstraction.\n\nNotification types:\n\n- booking created\n- booking confirmed\n- booking changed\n- booking cancelled\n- appointment reminder\n- review invitation\n\nInitial MVP may support:\n\n- in-app notifications\n- email through optional provider configuration\n\nPrepare interfaces for:\n\n- push notifications\n- SMS\n- WhatsApp where permitted\n\nExternal credentials must be configured through environment variables or secure provider settings.\n\nIf notification credentials are absent:\n\n- core booking must still work\n- notification capability should show unavailable\n- do not hardcode credentials\n- do not block completion unless notifications are explicitly mandatory\n\n---\n\n# 7. PAYMENTS\n\nPrepare payment architecture but keep payments optional in the first MVP.\n\nFuture use cases:\n\n- prepayment\n- deposit\n- full online payment\n- cancellation fee\n- gift certificate\n\nDo not implement a fake payment flow.\n\nIf no payment provider is configured:\n\n- booking without payment must remain possible\n- payment status should be `not_required`\n\nSuggested payment states:\n\n- not_required\n- pending\n- authorized\n- paid\n- failed\n- refunded\n- partially_refunded\n\nDo not store card data.\n\nUse a certified external payment provider when payments are later enabled.\n\n---\n\n# 8. DATA MODEL\n\nCreate a normalized persistent database model.\n\nMinimum entities:\n\n## User\n\n- id\n- email\n- password_hash\n- first_name\n- last_name\n- phone\n- role\n- avatar_url\n- is_active\n- created_at\n- updated_at\n\n## Salon\n\n- id\n- owner_id\n- name\n- description\n- phone\n- email\n- address\n- city\n- latitude\n- longitude\n- logo_url\n- cover_url\n- rating_average\n- rating_count\n- approval_status\n- is_active\n- created_at\n- updated_at\n\n## SalonBranch\n\n- id\n- salon_id\n- name\n- address\n- city\n- latitude\n- longitude\n- phone\n- timezone\n- is_active\n\n## Specialist\n\n- id\n- user_id\n- salon_branch_id\n- display_name\n- title\n- biography\n- experience_years\n- avatar_url\n- rating_average\n- rating_count\n- is_active\n\n## ServiceCategory\n\n- id\n- name\n- icon\n- display_order\n- is_active\n\n## Service\n\n- id\n- salon_branch_id\n- category_id\n- name\n- description\n- duration_minutes\n- price\n- currency\n- buffer_before_minutes\n- buffer_after_minutes\n- is_active\n\n## SpecialistService\n\n- specialist_id\n- service_id\n- custom_price\n- custom_duration\n- is_active\n\n## WorkingSchedule\n\n- id\n- specialist_id\n- weekday\n- start_time\n- end_time\n- is_active\n\n## ScheduleException\n\n- id\n- specialist_id\n- date\n- start_time\n- end_time\n- exception_type\n- reason\n\nException types:\n\n- day_off\n- custom_hours\n- blocked\n- break\n\n## Appointment\n\n- id\n- customer_id\n- salon_branch_id\n- specialist_id\n- service_id\n- start_at\n- end_at\n- timezone\n- price\n- currency\n- status\n- customer_note\n- salon_note\n- cancellation_reason\n- created_at\n- updated_at\n\n## FavoriteSalon\n\n- customer_id\n- salon_id\n- created_at\n\n## FavoriteSpecialist\n\n- customer_id\n- specialist_id\n- created_at\n\n## Review\n\n- id\n- appointment_id\n- customer_id\n- salon_id\n- specialist_id\n- rating\n- text\n- moderation_status\n- created_at\n- updated_at\n\n## Notification\n\n- id\n- user_id\n- type\n- title\n- body\n- read_at\n- created_at\n\n## AuditLog\n\n- id\n- actor_user_id\n- action\n- entity_type\n- entity_id\n- metadata\n- created_at\n\nAdd indexes and constraints for frequently queried fields.\n\nUse UTC internally.\n\nStore branch/user timezone separately.\n\n---\n\n# 9. API\n\nCreate a documented REST API or another justified mobile-compatible API.\n\nSuggested endpoint groups:\n\n- `/api/auth`\n- `/api/users`\n- `/api/salons`\n- `/api/branches`\n- `/api/specialists`\n- `/api/services`\n- `/api/availability`\n- `/api/appointments`\n- `/api/favorites`\n- `/api/reviews`\n- `/api/notifications`\n- `/api/manager`\n- `/api/admin`\n\nExamples:\n\n- register\n- login\n- refresh session\n- search salons\n- get salon details\n- get specialist details\n- get available slots\n- create appointment\n- reschedule appointment\n- cancel appointment\n- list customer appointments\n- manage salon schedule\n\nUse:\n\n- request validation\n- structured error responses\n- pagination\n- sorting\n- filtering\n- authentication\n- authorization\n\nGenerate API documentation.\n\n---\n\n# 10. TECHNOLOGY SELECTION\n\nChoose a maintainable cross-platform stack.\n\nPreferred mobile options:\n\n- React Native with Expo\n- Flutter\n\nSelect one based on the existing development environment and explain the choice.\n\nPreferred backend options:\n\n- FastAPI\n- another well-supported typed backend if strongly justified\n\nPreferred database:\n\n- PostgreSQL for production\n- SQLite may be allowed only for simple local MVP development\n\nThe architecture must support migration from local development to production.\n\nDo not create two unrelated mobile codebases for iOS and Android.\n\n---\n\n# 11. MOBILE ARCHITECTURE\n\nUse maintainable separation:\n\n- presentation/UI\n- application/use cases\n- domain logic\n- data/API layer\n- authentication\n- local cache\n- configuration\n\nImplement:\n\n- navigation\n- authenticated/unauthenticated route separation\n- API client\n- token/session handling\n- form validation\n- centralized error handling\n- loading states\n- empty states\n- retry behavior\n- secure local session storage\n\nDo not store authentication tokens in plain unprotected storage where secure platform storage is available.\n\n---\n\n# 12. UI DESIGN\n\nCreate an original premium beauty-industry visual identity.\n\nProduct name:\n\nBeauty Lip\n\nStyle:\n\n- elegant\n- clean\n- warm\n- premium\n- modern\n- feminine without becoming childish\n- suitable for different salon categories\n\nSuggested palette direction:\n\n- soft ivory or warm neutral background\n- deep plum/burgundy accent\n- muted rose accent\n- dark readable typography\n- restrained gold accent for premium elements\n\nAvoid:\n\n- excessive pink\n- glitter effects\n- low-contrast text\n- overcrowded screens\n- generic admin-dashboard design\n- copying another application’s UI\n\nUse:\n\n- rounded but not oversized cards\n- high-quality typography\n- clear booking actions\n- large touch targets\n- accessible contrast\n- consistent spacing\n- clear status colors\n\nSupport:\n\n- light theme initially\n- architecture for dark theme later\n\n---\n\n# 13. NAVIGATION\n\nSuggested customer bottom navigation:\n\n- Home\n- Search\n- Appointments\n- Favorites\n- Profile\n\nUse native-feeling navigation.\n\nBooking flow may use a stack/modal flow.\n\nDo not overcrowd the bottom navigation.\n\nSalon manager dashboard should use a separate authenticated management navigation.\n\n---\n\n# 14. LOCALIZATION\n\nPrepare localization from the beginning.\n\nInitial languages:\n\n- German\n- English\n- Russian\n\nDo not hardcode all UI strings inside components.\n\nUse translation keys.\n\nDates, times, currencies, and phone formats must follow locale.\n\n---\n\n# 15. ACCESSIBILITY\n\nImplement:\n\n- semantic labels\n- screen-reader-friendly controls\n- sufficient contrast\n- scalable text\n- touch targets of appropriate size\n- keyboard accessibility for web management interface\n- clear validation messages\n- status not represented only by color\n\n---\n\n# 16. SECURITY AND PRIVACY\n\nImplement:\n\n- secure password hashing\n- authentication tokens or secure sessions\n- role authorization\n- rate limiting for authentication\n- input validation\n- protection against common injection attacks\n- safe file-upload validation\n- audit logs for management actions\n- secret configuration through environment variables\n- no hardcoded credentials\n\nPrepare for GDPR requirements:\n\n- consent where required\n- privacy policy link\n- export account data\n- account deletion request\n- retention policy\n- minimal personal-data collection\n\nDo not claim full GDPR compliance without an actual legal and operational review.\n\n---\n\n# 17. IMAGE UPLOADS\n\nSupport images for:\n\n- salon logo\n- salon gallery\n- specialist avatar\n- specialist portfolio\n\nValidate:\n\n- MIME type\n- extension\n- file size\n- image dimensions\n\nDo not trust filenames.\n\nUse configurable local storage for development and object storage abstraction for production.\n\n---\n\n# 18. EMPTY, ERROR, AND LOADING STATES\n\nEvery main screen must include proper states:\n\n- loading\n- no data\n- network error\n- authentication expired\n- no availability\n- no search results\n- no appointments\n- no favorites\n\nDo not leave blank screens.\n\n---\n\n# 19. DEMO AND SEED DATA\n\nProvide development seed data:\n\n- several salons\n- several specialists\n- several services\n- working schedules\n- available and occupied slots\n- demo customer account\n- demo salon-manager account\n- demo administrator account\n\nCredentials must be clearly marked as development-only.\n\nDo not enable default demo credentials in production configuration.\n\n---\n\n# 20. TESTING\n\nCreate automated tests for critical business behavior.\n\nBackend tests:\n\n- registration and login\n- role authorization\n- salon search\n- service retrieval\n- availability calculation\n- booking creation\n- double-booking prevention\n- rescheduling\n- cancellation\n- persistence\n- review permission\n- manager access restrictions\n- administrator access restrictions\n\nMobile tests:\n\n- authentication flow\n- salon list loading\n- salon details\n- booking flow\n- appointments list\n- cancellation confirmation\n- form validation\n- network-error state\n\nEnd-to-end flow:\n\n1. Customer logs in.\n2. Customer finds a salon.\n3. Customer chooses a service.\n4. Customer chooses a specialist.\n5. Customer selects an available time.\n6. Customer confirms booking.\n7. Appointment appears in customer application.\n8. Appointment appears in salon manager calendar.\n9. Manager confirms appointment.\n10. Customer sees updated status.\n11. Customer reschedules or cancels according to policy.\n12. Double booking is rejected.\n\n---\n\n# 21. DOCUMENTATION\n\nProvide:\n\n- project architecture overview\n- local development instructions\n- backend startup instructions\n- mobile startup instructions\n- environment variable template\n- database migration instructions\n- seed-data instructions\n- test commands\n- Android development instructions\n- iOS development instructions\n- production-build notes\n- known limitations\n\nDocument exact commands for Windows where applicable.\n\nDo not claim iOS builds can be produced locally on Windows without the required Apple/macOS tooling.\n\n---\n\n# 22. DELIVERY ARTIFACTS\n\nThe project must contain:\n\n- mobile application source\n- backend source\n- salon management interface\n- database models and migrations\n- environment example\n- seed data\n- automated tests\n- README\n- architecture documentation\n- API documentation\n- build/run scripts where appropriate\n\nDo not deliver only screenshots or static mockups.\n\n---\n\n# 23. IMPLEMENTATION PHASES\n\nImplement in controlled phases.\n\n## Phase 1 — Foundation\n\n- repository structure\n- selected technology stack\n- configuration\n- database\n- authentication\n- role model\n- basic mobile navigation\n- basic management interface\n\n## Phase 2 — Salon Catalog\n\n- salons\n- branches\n- specialists\n- services\n- search\n- filters\n- profiles\n\n## Phase 3 — Booking Engine\n\n- schedules\n- availability\n- appointment creation\n- double-booking prevention\n- rescheduling\n- cancellation\n\n## Phase 4 — Customer Experience\n\n- appointments\n- favorites\n- reviews\n- notifications\n- profile\n\n## Phase 5 — Salon Management\n\n- dashboard\n- calendar\n- appointment management\n- specialist management\n- service management\n- customers\n- analytics\n\n## Phase 6 — Hardening\n\n- security\n- tests\n- error states\n- localization\n- performance\n- documentation\n- E2E validation\n\nComplete and test each phase before moving to the next.\n\n---\n\n# 24. MVP PRIORITY\n\nThe MVP is complete when the following real workflow works:\n\nCustomer:\n\n- registers or logs in\n- searches salons\n- opens salon profile\n- selects service\n- selects specialist\n- sees real availability\n- books appointment\n- sees appointment in profile\n- reschedules or cancels\n\nSalon manager:\n\n- logs in\n- sees appointment\n- confirms or updates status\n- manages specialists, services, and schedule\n\nSystem:\n\n- persists all data\n- prevents double booking\n- enforces roles\n- survives restart\n- passes automated tests\n\nFeatures such as payments, SMS, advanced analytics, loyalty programs, and promotional campaigns may remain documented future extensions.\n\n---\n\n# 25. DO NOT\n\nDo not:\n\n- copy another application’s UI or branding\n- create a static-only prototype\n- hardcode demo data into production screens\n- hardcode API keys\n- store plain-text passwords\n- allow double booking\n- allow customers to access management functions\n- mark unavailable time as bookable\n- implement fake payments\n- make notification credentials mandatory for core booking\n- use one giant unmaintainable application file\n- skip mobile error and empty states\n- claim production readiness without tests and documentation\n\n---\n\n# 26. REQUIRED FINAL REPORT\n\nAt completion report:\n\n- selected technology stack and justification\n- mobile architecture\n- backend architecture\n- database schema\n- implemented roles\n- implemented customer screens\n- implemented salon-management screens\n- booking and availability logic\n- double-booking protection\n- authentication and authorization\n- notifications status\n- localization status\n- automated test results\n- mobile build result\n- backend startup result\n- management UI build result\n- E2E booking result\n- files and major directories created\n- environment variables required\n- known limitations\n- next recommended phase\n\nFinal conclusion must be one of:\n\nBEAUTY LIP MVP PASSED\n\nor:\n\nBEAUTY LIB MVP FAILED\n\nDo not return PASSED unless the complete customer booking and salon-management E2E workflow has been executed successfully.\n\n---\n\n# CREDENTIAL AND INTEGRATION STRATEGY\n\nDo not ask the user for real secrets during generation. Do not hardcode, invent, print, or log secret values.\n\nUse environment variables only and provide a complete .env.example with placeholder names, including:\n\n- DATABASE_URL\n- STRIPE_API_KEY\n- STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET\n- SMTP_HOST\n- SMTP_PORT\n- SMTP_USERNAME\n- SMTP_PASSWORD\n- SMTP_FROM_EMAIL\n- JWT_SECRET\n\nFor the MVP, implement safe local defaults:\n\n- database: SQLite fallback when DATABASE_URL is not provided\n- payments: mock payment provider / disabled Stripe mode when STRIPE_API_KEY is not provided\n- emails/reminders: console/log notification provider when SMTP settings are not provided\n- authentication: generated local development JWT secret fallback only for local development, documented clearly\n\nThe app must remain runnable and testable without external credentials. External production integrations must be isolated behind configuration adapters and documented in README.\n\nTests must mock Stripe, SMTP, push notifications, and other network calls. Never perform real external calls in tests.\n",
        "project_goal": "Beauty Lib - Relaunch",
